{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"array-methods","title":"map, filter & reduce","group":"Arrays & Objects","order":22,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">The three workhorses of functional JavaScript: transform every item, keep some items, fold everything into one value — without mutating the original.</p>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrow-right\"></i>map — transform each element</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const prices = [10, 20, 30];\nconst withTax = prices.map((p) =&gt; p * 1.1);\nconsole.log(withTax.map((p) =&gt; p.toFixed(2)));\nconst users = [{ name: \"Asha\", age: 28 }, { name: \"Ravi\", age: 17 }];\nconsole.log(users.map(({ name }) =&gt; name));\nconsole.log(users.map((u, i) =&gt; `${i + 1}. ${u.name}`));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[ '11.00', '22.00', '33.00' ]\n[ 'Asha', 'Ravi' ]\n[ '1. Asha', '2. Ravi' ]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-filter\"></i>filter — keep what passes the test</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const nums = [5, 12, 8, 130, 44];\nconsole.log(nums.filter((n) =&gt; n &gt; 10));\nconst mixed = [0, \"a\", null, \"\", 7, undefined, false];\nconsole.log(mixed.filter(Boolean));          // remove falsy values\nconst unique = [1, 2, 2, 3, 1].filter((x, i, arr) =&gt; arr.indexOf(x) === i);\nconsole.log(unique);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[ 12, 130, 44 ]\n[ 'a', 7 ]\n[ 1, 2, 3 ]</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-join\"></i>reduce — fold into a single value</h3>\n<div class=\"formula\"><code>array.reduce((accumulator, current, index, array) =&gt; newAccumulator, initialValue)</code></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const nums = [1, 2, 3, 4];\nconsole.log(nums.reduce((sum, n) =&gt; sum + n, 0));\nconsole.log(nums.reduce((max, n) =&gt; (n &gt; max ? n : max), -Infinity));\n\nconst words = [\"apple\", \"avocado\", \"banana\", \"cherry\", \"blueberry\"];\nconst byLetter = words.reduce((groups, w) =&gt; {\n  (groups[w[0]] ??= []).push(w);\n  return groups;\n}, {});\nconsole.log(byLetter);\n\nconst pairs = [[\"a\", 1], [\"b\", 2]];\nconsole.log(pairs.reduce((obj, [k, v]) =&gt; ({ ...obj, [k]: v }), {}));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>10\n4\n{\n  a: [ 'apple', 'avocado' ],\n  b: [ 'banana', 'blueberry' ],\n  c: [ 'cherry' ]\n}\n{ a: 1, b: 2 }</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Always pass the initial value. Without it, <code>reduce</code> uses the first element as the accumulator — and throws <code>TypeError: Reduce of empty array with no initial value</code> on <code>[]</code>.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>try {\n  [].reduce((a, b) =&gt; a + b);\n} catch (e) {\n  console.log(e.message);\n}\nconsole.log([].reduce((a, b) =&gt; a + b, 0));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>Reduce of empty array with no initial value\n0</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i>Chaining</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const orders = [\n  { item: \"book\", qty: 2, price: 12, paid: true },\n  { item: \"pen\", qty: 10, price: 1.5, paid: false },\n  { item: \"lamp\", qty: 1, price: 40, paid: true },\n];\nconst revenue = orders\n  .filter((o) =&gt; o.paid)\n  .map((o) =&gt; o.qty * o.price)\n  .reduce((sum, v) =&gt; sum + v, 0);\nconsole.log(revenue);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>64</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list\"></i>forEach and flatMap</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const out = [];\nconst r = [\"x\", \"y\"].forEach((v, i) =&gt; out.push(v + i));\nconsole.log(out, r);          // forEach always returns undefined\nconst sentences = [\"hello world\", \"hi there\"];\nconsole.log(sentences.flatMap((s) =&gt; s.split(\" \")));\nconsole.log([1, 2, 3].flatMap((n) =&gt; (n % 2 ? [n, n * 10] : [])));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>[ 'x0', 'y1' ] undefined\n[ 'hello', 'world', 'hi', 'there' ]\n[ 1, 10, 3, 30 ]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Returns</th><th>Use when</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>map</code></td><td>New array, same length</td><td>Transforming each item</td></tr><tr><td><code>filter</code></td><td>New array, ≤ length</td><td>Selecting items</td></tr><tr><td><code>reduce</code></td><td>Any single value</td><td>Sums, grouping, building objects</td></tr><tr><td><code>forEach</code></td><td><code>undefined</code></td><td>Side effects only (logging, DOM)</td></tr><tr><td><code>flatMap</code></td><td>New array, flattened one level</td><td>Map where each item yields 0..n items</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between map and forEach?</summary><div><p><code>map</code> returns a new array of the callback's return values and is chainable; <code>forEach</code> returns <code>undefined</code> and exists only for side effects. Neither can be stopped early with <code>break</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Implement map using reduce.</summary><div><p><code>const map = (arr, fn) =&gt; arr.reduce((acc, x, i) =&gt; (acc.push(fn(x, i, arr)), acc), []);</code> — push each transformed item into the accumulator array.</p></div></details></section>"}